# koom — production deployment environment variables template
#
# Copy this file to web/.env.prod.local and fill in the blanks. Both
# files live in the `web/` directory; `.env.prod.local` is gitignored
# while this template is committed. The operator and complete doctor
# modes (`npm run doctor` and `npm run doctor:all`) auto-create
# `.env.prod.local` from this template when needed. `doctor:dev`
# deliberately does not create or read this production-only file.
#
# ── Why a separate file from .env.local? ──
#
# `.env.local` holds values needed for LOCAL DEVELOPMENT. The values
# in this file are only useful for VALIDATING and DEPLOYING TO
# PRODUCTION from your dev machine. Keeping them separate means:
#
#   - `.env.local` contains local settings plus the shared R2/admin
#     values that act as operator tooling's source of truth.
#   - This file contains only production-control credentials; the
#     Next.js dev server never loads it.
#   - The focused doctor commands validate local development and
#     operator readiness without asking you to swap values or satisfy
#     requirements from the other path.
#   - `npm run render:sync` and the doctor have a single, unambiguous
#     file to read production-only settings from.
#
# Nothing in this file is deployed. Render gets its configuration from
# render.yaml plus the values you paste when creating the Blueprint.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Render
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Step 1: Deploy the Blueprint.
#         Push the koom repo to GitHub, then in the Render dashboard
#         choose New → Blueprint and pick the repo. Render reads
#         render.yaml, creates the koom-db Postgres instance and the
#         koom-web service, and prompts once for each `sync: false`
#         variable. Paste the matching values from web/.env.local:
#           - GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET, KOOM_ADMIN_EMAILS
#           - R2_BUCKET, R2_ACCOUNT_ID, R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID,
#             R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL
#         DATABASE_URL and KOOM_SESSION_SECRET are set up by Render
#         automatically, and share URLs use the service's onrender.com
#         URL unless you set KOOM_PUBLIC_BASE_URL.
#
# Step 2: Create an API key.
#         Render dashboard → Account Settings → API Keys. Paste it into
#         RENDER_API_KEY below. The doctor and `npm run render:sync`
#         only ever read through it.
#
# Step 3: Copy the service id.
#         Open the koom-web service; its URL ends in the `srv-...` id.
#         Paste that into RENDER_SERVICE_ID below.
#
# Step 4: Copy the external database URL.
#         Open koom-db → Connect → External Database URL. Paste it into
#         RENDER_DATABASE_URL below so the doctor can verify the
#         production schema. The web service itself uses the internal
#         URL; this one is only for tooling on your machine.

RENDER_API_KEY=
RENDER_SERVICE_ID=
RENDER_DATABASE_URL=
